{
  "abstract": "Lifting the modular hyperbola $xy\\equiv k\\pmod p$ periodically gives the best known asymptotic lower bound $(3/2-o(1))n$ for the no-three-in-line problem. Recent work shows that one hyperbola, and every nonsingular conic, stays below the $1.8n$ target in every window (for conics, every window of side $n\\ge p$), and that every arc modulo $p$ (a residue set with no three collinear points) gives at most $3(p-1)$ points in the two-period window. We remove the remaining restriction on the window side for arbitrary, possibly non-conic arcs. For every prime $p\\ge5$, every arc $R\\subseteq\\mathbb{F}_p^2$, every window position and every side $p\\le n<4p$, every no-three-in-line subset of the lift of $R$ has fewer than $\\tfrac95n$ points, and asymptotically at most $(\\tfrac85+O(1/p))n$. For nine primes $13\\le p\\le47$ the bound holds for every $n\\ge p$. The proof splits by whether a residue row or column holds two points. If one does, counting integer rows with Segre's bound on tangents through a point at infinity gives an explicit closed form. If none does, the lifts of distinct residues meet only along slope $\\pm1$ diagonals, where each point has at most one partner in each direction, so the arc decomposes into alternating chains. We bound every chain by a finite certificate verified with exact integer longest-path computations in two independent implementations, with rates $3/4$, $4/5$ and $79/100$ per unit of the row-plus-column lift count for windows of one to four periods. Segre's bound limits the number of chains, and an exact rational optimization yields $8/5$. Exact CP-SAT solutions of 235 random instances with all lines enforced respect every intermediate bound. Short windows $n<p$ and $p=3$ genuinely fail, and residue sets with collinear triples are not covered. The result constrains a construction family; it does not resolve the $1.8n$ problem.",
